% ============================================================================
% ocean-zone-depth — each of the ocean's first three depth zones → the
% approximate depth in meters the SAME WHOI source states it reaches down
% to (adj-facts-stdlib, OCEANOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `ocean-zones.adj` (`ocean_zone(zone,
% order)`, ONE ordinal position per zone: sunlight_zone=1, twilight_zone=2,
% midnight_zone=3): a new `ocean_zone_depth(zone, max_depth_meters)` table
% names the approximate depth in meters the SAME already-cited WHOI "Ocean
% Zones" page states for each zone, decoded from spans already sitting
% unused inside `ocean-zones.adj`'s own header truth table — no new
% WebFetch. That table's `order` column has room for only the SEQUENCE
% position; the SAME already-quoted WHOI sentences also carry a depth
% figure for each zone that never made it into a row:
%
%   sunlight_zone → 200
%     "sunlight penetrates only to depths of about 200 meters (656 feet)"
%   twilight_zone → 1000
%     "It lies 200 to 1,000 meters below the ocean surface"
%   midnight_zone → 4000
%     "The midnight zone, or bathypelagic, extends to about 4,000 meters
%      (about 13,100 feet)"
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ocean_zone_depth(twilight_zone, $MaxDepthMeters)      % 1000
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `ocean-zones.adj`'s own `ocean_zone`): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `ocean_zone_depth(zone, max_depth_meters)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the depth WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any zone that is not one of the three tabled here
% (it never invents a depth). The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a depth
% and recall which zone reaches down to it:
%
%     ? ocean_zone_depth($Zone, 4000)   % midnight_zone
%
% Each value is the approximate LOWER boundary depth the source states for
% that zone (sunlight zone's own depth is a single ceiling figure;
% twilight and midnight zones' quotes give a range or "extends to" figure,
% and this table tables the DEEPER end of each, matching the ordering
% already fixed by the parent table's `order` column: 200 < 1000 < 4000).
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME sunlight-zone span
% already quoted inside `ocean-zones.adj`'s own header/provenance section,
% from the SAME Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution (WHOI) "Ocean Zones"
% page that table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different
% schema decoding a part of each already-verified quote. An ADJ `table`
% carries ONE provenance envelope (per `ocean-zones.adj`'s own
% convention), so `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the sunlight-zone
% span; the twilight/midnight rows' own spans are listed above for
% independent auditability. `trust consensus` — the same tier
% `ocean-zones.adj` already carries (WHOI is a reputable, long-running
% oceanographic research institution but not a .gov domain).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ocean_zone_depth {
    columns zone, max_depth_meters

    row (sunlight_zone, 200)
    row (twilight_zone, 1000)
    row (midnight_zone, 4000)

    source "Because water strongly absorbs light, sunlight penetrates only to depths of about 200 meters (656 feet)."
    locator "https://www.whoi.edu/know-your-ocean/ocean-topics/how-the-ocean-works/ocean-zones/"
    trust consensus
}
